## {.center}

::: {.r-fit-text}
What do those metronomes,\
a room full of people clapping,\
and the cells that keep your heart beating\
have in common?
:::

::: {.notes}
0:00-2:00 -- COLD OPEN. Start the physical metronomes ("Metronomes in
Step") running *before* you say anything -- let them tick, visibly out
of step, while the room settles. Don't explain them yet. Ask this
question and leave it hanging. No agenda, no "let me tell you about
myself."
:::

---

::: {.r-fit-text}
1665.

Two pendulum clocks.

One wall.
:::

::: {.notes}
2:00-7:00 (story 1 of 4) -- Christiaan Huygens, bedridden and bored,
notices two pendulum clocks hanging on the same wall drift into
perfect lockstep -- and drift back out the moment you separate them.
Metronomes still ticking in the background the whole time. You've
planted them; you haven't returned to them yet.
:::

---

::: {.r-fit-text}
A riverbank.

Thousands of fireflies.

No leader.
:::

::: {.notes}
Story 2 -- fireflies in parts of Southeast Asia flashing in unison
across a whole riverbank, with no leader.
:::

---

::: {.r-fit-text}
Applause --

ragged at first.

Then, somehow, one clap.
:::

::: {.notes}
Story 3 -- an audience's applause, ragged at first, occasionally
slipping into a single unified clap.
:::

---

::: {.r-fit-text}
Your heart.

Thousands of unreliable cells.

One beat. Every second. Your whole life.
:::

::: {.notes}
Story 4 -- pacemaker cells in your heart, thousands of individually
unreliable cells, somehow agreeing on one beat, every second, for
your entire life. This closes the story run -- next slide names it.
:::

---

## Coupled oscillator theory

### the Kuramoto model

::: {.notes}
7:00-9:00 -- THE TURN. Name it: this is mathematics, a real active
research area. Check the metronomes here, live, in front of the room.
If they haven't locked in step yet: say so, let the tension sit
("they're not there yet -- watch"). If they HAVE synced already,
that's a genuine "it happened while we were talking" moment --
better than anything staged.
:::

---

::: {.r-fit-text}
Watch it happen.
:::

::: {.notes}
9:00-18:00 -- CENTREPIECE, longest section by design, don't rush once
running. Switch away from the slides to the "Sync Dial" simulator
(link: <https://claude.ai/code/artifact/c75c561c-d561-4a30-93f9-c7da9d5c65ff>)
on the shared screen. Drag the coupling slider K from low to high,
live, and let the room watch the order parameter r climb from near 0
toward 1 as the oscillators lock into phase. Come back to this deck
for the next slide once the room has seen it happen.
:::

---

### Before

::: {layout-ncol="1"}
<svg viewBox="0 0 300 300" width="380" style="display:block;margin:0 auto;">
  <circle cx="150" cy="150" r="120" fill="none" stroke="#666" stroke-dasharray="4 4"/>
  <circle cx="150" cy="150" r="3" fill="#e8e8e8"/>
  <circle cx="267" cy="125" r="8" fill="#4fd1c5"/>
  <circle cx="181" cy="34"  r="8" fill="#4fd1c5"/>
  <circle cx="61"  cy="70"  r="8" fill="#4fd1c5"/>
  <circle cx="31"  cy="133" r="8" fill="#4fd1c5"/>
  <circle cx="46"  cy="210" r="8" fill="#4fd1c5"/>
  <circle cx="119" cy="266" r="8" fill="#4fd1c5"/>
  <circle cx="206" cy="256" r="8" fill="#4fd1c5"/>
  <circle cx="259" cy="201" r="8" fill="#4fd1c5"/>
</svg>
:::

Eight independent phases, scattered.\
Order parameter *r* ≈ 0.

::: {.notes}
Each dot is one oscillator's phase, plotted on the unit circle in the
complex plane. Scattered around the circle -> their average (the
order parameter) sits near the centre -> r close to 0 -> no
synchrony. This is the metronomes/fireflies/applause before they
fall into step.
:::

---

### After

::: {layout-ncol="1"}
<svg viewBox="0 0 300 300" width="380" style="display:block;margin:0 auto;">
  <circle cx="150" cy="150" r="120" fill="none" stroke="#666" stroke-dasharray="4 4"/>
  <line x1="150" y1="150" x2="228" y2="72" stroke="#f6ad55" stroke-width="5" marker-end="url(#arrow)"/>
  <circle cx="248" cy="81" r="7" fill="#4fd1c5"/>
  <circle cx="242" cy="73" r="7" fill="#4fd1c5"/>
  <circle cx="239" cy="70" r="7" fill="#4fd1c5"/>
  <circle cx="235" cy="65" r="7" fill="#4fd1c5"/>
  <circle cx="232" cy="62" r="7" fill="#4fd1c5"/>
  <circle cx="227" cy="58" r="7" fill="#4fd1c5"/>
  <circle cx="222" cy="54" r="7" fill="#4fd1c5"/>
  <circle cx="214" cy="48" r="7" fill="#4fd1c5"/>
  <defs>
    <marker id="arrow" markerWidth="10" markerHeight="10" refX="6" refY="3" orient="auto">
      <path d="M0,0 L6,3 L0,6 Z" fill="#f6ad55"/>
    </marker>
  </defs>
</svg>
:::

Eight phases, locked together.\
Order parameter *r* ≈ 0.95.

::: {.notes}
Same eight oscillators, now clustered at nearly the same phase.
Their average -- the bold arrow -- now reaches almost to the edge of
the circle. r near 1: synchrony. Connect verbally back to the
metronomes on the table: same mathematics, two different machines.
:::

---

::: {.r-fit-text}
$$\frac{d\theta_i}{dt} = \omega_i + \frac{K}{N}\sum_{j=1}^{N} \sin(\theta_j - \theta_i)$$
:::

$$r\,e^{i\psi} = \frac{1}{N}\sum_{j=1}^{N} e^{i\theta_j}$$

::: {.notes}
Introduce this only *after* the room has watched it happen on the
Sync Dial. Frame it as "here's the four-line description of what you
just watched" -- not a thing to be understood, just recognised.
Top: how fast oscillator i's phase moves -- its own natural rhythm
omega_i, plus a pull from everyone else, strength K. Bottom: r is
literally the two diagrams you just saw, written as one complex
number.
:::

---

::: {.r-fit-text}
Same mathematics.

Two different machines.
:::

::: {.notes}
Point back at the physical metronomes on the table -- ticking in step
now, if the timing has worked. Leave slack in this whole centrepiece
section; live demos are where talks go long, and that's fine here.
:::

